Relevant columns and their potential usage
• rental.rental_id : primary key that lets us count “orders” (one row = one rental order)  
• rental.rental_date : extract YEAR() and strftime('%m') (or SUBSTR) to obtain the year / month of each order  
• rental.inventory_id : foreign‑key that points to inventory, so we can discover in which store the film copy was located when it was rented  
• inventory.store_id : identifies the store that owns the inventory item, therefore the store that actually made the rental  
• staff.staff_id : staff who processed the rental (not strictly needed for the requested ranking, but can be used in a JOIN filter if we wanted to be sure rentals were made by that store’s members)  
• store.store_id : master list of stores; useful when we want to be sure every store appears in the result set, even those with no rentals during some periods  

Typical conditions we may apply
• JOIN rental     ON rental.inventory_id = inventory.inventory_id  
• JOIN staff      ON staff.staff_id = rental.staff_id       (optional)  
• JOIN store      ON store.store_id = inventory.store_id  
• WHERE rental.rental_date IS NOT NULL  
• GROUP BY inventory.store_id,
          strftime('%Y', rental.rental_date),
          strftime('%m', rental.rental_date)  
• ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC  

Ten exploratory SQLite queries  
(They are ordered from very simple inspection to the final query that delivers the requested “year‑month with the highest number of rentals for each store”.  Each one is limited to 20 rows so it can run safely in any environment.)

```sql
-- 1) Description: See the list of existing stores (IDs only)
SELECT DISTINCT "store_id"
FROM "store"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: See staff members and to which store they belong
SELECT "staff_id",
       "first_name",
       "last_name",
       "store_id"
FROM   "staff"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Sample rentals together with the store that generated them
SELECT r."rental_id",
       r."rental_date",
       i."store_id"
FROM   "rental"  AS r
JOIN   "inventory" AS i
       ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Distinct rental years present in the data
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year"
FROM   "rental" AS r
ORDER BY "year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Distinct year‑month combinations for one store (e.g., store 1)
SELECT DISTINCT i."store_id",
       strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year",
       strftime('%m', r."rental_date") AS "month"
FROM   "rental"    AS r
JOIN   "inventory" AS i
       ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
WHERE  i."store_id" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Count rentals per store in total
SELECT i."store_id",
       COUNT(*)      AS "total_rentals"
FROM   "rental"    AS r
JOIN   "inventory" AS i
       ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
GROUP  BY i."store_id"
ORDER  BY "total_rentals" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Count rentals per store per year‑month
SELECT i."store_id",
       strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year",
       strftime('%m', r."rental_date") AS "month",
       COUNT(*)                       AS "rentals_in_month"
FROM   "rental"    AS r
JOIN   "inventory" AS i
       ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
GROUP  BY i."store_id",
          strftime('%Y', r."rental_date"),
          strftime('%m', r."rental_date")
ORDER  BY i."store_id",
          "rentals_in_month" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: For each store, find the maximum monthly rental count
SELECT i."store_id",
       MAX(monthly_ct) AS "max_monthly_rentals"
FROM  (
        SELECT i."store_id",
               strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year",
               strftime('%m', r."rental_date") AS "month",
               COUNT(*)                       AS monthly_ct
        FROM   "rental"    AS r
        JOIN   "inventory" AS i
               ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
        GROUP  BY i."store_id",
                  strftime('%Y', r."rental_date"),
                  strftime('%m', r."rental_date")
      ) AS x
GROUP BY i."store_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Bring together year, month and rental count, then rank rows per store
SELECT i."store_id",
       strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year",
       strftime('%m', r."rental_date") AS "month",
       COUNT(*)                       AS "rentals_in_month"
FROM   "rental"    AS r
JOIN   "inventory" AS i
       ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
GROUP  BY i."store_id",
          strftime('%Y', r."rental_date"),
          strftime('%m', r."rental_date")
ORDER  BY i."store_id",
          "rentals_in_month" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: FINAL – year & month with the highest rental count for each store
SELECT o."store_id",
       o."year",
       o."month",
       o."rentals_in_month" AS "total_rentals"
FROM (
     SELECT i."store_id",
            strftime('%Y', r."rental_date") AS "year",
            strftime('%m', r."rental_date") AS "month",
            COUNT(*)                       AS "rentals_in_month",
            ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY i."store_id"
               ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
            ) AS rn
     FROM   "rental"    AS r
     JOIN   "inventory" AS i
            ON i."inventory_id" = r."inventory_id"
     GROUP  BY i."store_id",
              strftime('%Y', r."rental_date"),
              strftime('%m', r."rental_date")
) AS o
WHERE o.rn = 1
ORDER BY o."store_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query helps to understand the data better and culminates in Query 10, which returns, for every store, the (year, month) that generated the greatest number of rental orders together with that total.
